Claim 1: Being diagnosed with ADHD means being "mentally ill," and calling a diagnosed student "a mentally ill person"
→ Unsupported/mischaracterized — ADHD is classified in ICD-11 under "Neurodevelopmental disorders," a distinct diagnostic category from what is colloquially meant by "mental illness" in this dismissive/pejorative sense; none of the attached references frame ADHD this way, and using the label as an insult misrepresents the clinical classification

Claim 2: ADHD medication ("rainbow magical drugs") "doesn't do anything" for the person taking it
→ Unsupported — directly contradicted by NICE, which presents stimulant and non-stimulant medications as evidence-based treatments with documented effects on symptoms and impairment (1.7.7–1.7.19), including dose titration guided by "reduced symptoms, positive behaviour change, improvements in education, employment and relationships" (1.7.27)

Claim 3: A person with ADHD can have genuine difficulty focusing/processing information in a lecture setting (e.g., large amounts of text on a screen, monotone speech), warranting tools like recording lectures and note-taking supports
→ Supported — matches ICD-11 inattention criteria: "having difficulty sustaining attention on tasks that do not provide a high level of stimulation," "being easily distracted," and "having difficulty planning, managing and organizing schoolwork, tasks and other activities"; NICE also explicitly supports "reasonable adjustments" in educational settings (1.4.3, 1.4.12) as appropriate accommodations for ADHD

Claim 4: Referring to ADHD accommodations/awareness dismissively as "ADHD propaganda"
→ Unsupported/mischaracterized — this rhetorical framing contradicts NICE's guidance, which explicitly recommends reasonable educational adjustments (1.4.12) and treats ADHD as a legitimate, evidence-based neurodevelopmental diagnosis requiring structured clinical assessment (1.3.1), not an invented or exaggerated narrative

